            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "enabled": true,
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Possible indication of Solorigate. Detects a modified FNV1 function, where there is an additional xor using a literal after the regular FNV hash"
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# Detects a modified FNV function\nIn Solorigate, the malicious code tried to evade various security detection software by comparing hashes of the process names against an embedded list of values. The malicious code used in the SolarWinds attack included hash values calculated using a standard FNV-1A 64-bit hash with an additional XOR by a literal after computing the FNV-1A.\n\nThis query detects FNV-like hash calculations where there is an additional XOR (with any static value) after the hash calculation loop.\n\nThe nation-state supply chain attack on SolarWinds known as Solorigate or SunBurst gave nation-state actors access to some victims' networks.\n\nThe purpose of these rules is to identify potentially tampered code that requires further analysis.\n\n\n## Recommendation\nAny findings from this rule are only intended to indicate suspicious code that shares similarities with known portions of code used for the Solorigate attack. There is no certainty that the code is related or that the code is part of any attack.\n\nFor more information, please visit https://aka.ms/solorigate.\n\n",
                "text": "# Detects a modified FNV function\nIn Solorigate, the malicious code tried to evade various security detection software by comparing hashes of the process names against an embedded list of values. The malicious code used in the SolarWinds attack included hash values calculated using a standard FNV-1A 64-bit hash with an additional XOR by a literal after computing the FNV-1A.\n\nThis query detects FNV-like hash calculations where there is an additional XOR (with any static value) after the hash calculation loop.\n\nThe nation-state supply chain attack on SolarWinds known as Solorigate or SunBurst gave nation-state actors access to some victims' networks.\n\nThe purpose of these rules is to identify potentially tampered code that requires further analysis.\n\n\n## Recommendation\nAny findings from this rule are only intended to indicate suspicious code that shares similarities with known portions of code used for the Solorigate attack. There is no certainty that the code is related or that the code is part of any attack.\n\nFor more information, please visit https://aka.ms/solorigate.\n\n"
              },
              "id": "cs/solorigate/modified-fnv-function-detection",
              "name": "cs/solorigate/modified-fnv-function-detection",
              "properties": {
                "description": "Possible indication of Solorigate. Detects a modified FNV1 function, where there is an additional xor using a literal after the regular FNV hash",
                "id": "cs/solorigate/modified-fnv-function-detection",
                "kind": "problem",
                "name": "Detects a modified FNV function",
                "precision": "high",
                "problem.severity": "error",
                "queryURI": "https://github.com/github/codeql/blob/a1cdf256ad6b7c3e9984db9069671647e5f47921/csharp/ql/src/experimental/Security%20Features/campaign/Solorigate/ModifiedFnvFunctionDetection.ql",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "solorigate"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Detects a modified FNV function"
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "enabled": true,
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The total number of Solorigate-related literals found in the code is above a threshold, which may indicate that an external agent has tampered with the code. It is recommended to review the code and verify that there is no unexpected code in this project."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# Number of Solorigate-related literals is above the threshold\nThis query detects literals used in the Solorigate code.\n\nThis query detects when the code includes at least 30 of the literals used in the Solorigate tampered code\n\nPlease notice that by themselves these literals are not malign.\n\nThe nation-state supply chain attack on SolarWinds known as Solorigate or SunBurst gave nation-state actors access to some victims' networks.\n\nThe purpose of these rules is to identify potentially tampered code that requires further analysis.\n\n\n## Recommendation\nAny findings from this rule are only intended to indicate suspicious code that shares similarities with known portions of code used for the Solorigate attack. There is no certainty that the code is related or that the code is part of any attack.\n\nFor more information, please visit https://aka.ms/solorigate.\n\n",
                "text": "# Number of Solorigate-related literals is above the threshold\nThis query detects literals used in the Solorigate code.\n\nThis query detects when the code includes at least 30 of the literals used in the Solorigate tampered code\n\nPlease notice that by themselves these literals are not malign.\n\nThe nation-state supply chain attack on SolarWinds known as Solorigate or SunBurst gave nation-state actors access to some victims' networks.\n\nThe purpose of these rules is to identify potentially tampered code that requires further analysis.\n\n\n## Recommendation\nAny findings from this rule are only intended to indicate suspicious code that shares similarities with known portions of code used for the Solorigate attack. There is no certainty that the code is related or that the code is part of any attack.\n\nFor more information, please visit https://aka.ms/solorigate.\n\n"
              },
              "id": "cs/solorigate/number-of-known-literals-above-threshold",
              "name": "cs/solorigate/number-of-known-literals-above-threshold",
              "properties": {
                "description": "The total number of Solorigate-related literals found in the code is above a threshold, which may indicate that an external agent has tampered with the code.\n      It is recommended to review the code and verify that there is no unexpected code in this project.",
                "id": "cs/solorigate/number-of-known-literals-above-threshold",
                "kind": "problem",
                "name": "Number of Solorigate-related literals is above the threshold",
                "precision": "medium",
                "problem.severity": "warning",
                "queryURI": "https://github.com/github/codeql/blob/a1cdf256ad6b7c3e9984db9069671647e5f47921/csharp/ql/src/experimental/Security%20Features/campaign/Solorigate/NumberOfKnownLiteralsAboveThreshold.ql",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "solorigate"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Number of Solorigate-related literals is above the threshold"
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "enabled": true,
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The total number of Solorigate-related hash literals found in the code is above a threshold, which may indicate that an external agent has tampered with the code. It is recommended to review the code and verify that there is no unexpected code in this project, however it is highly unlikely the hash values would be present coincidentally."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# Number of Solorigate-related hashes as literals is above the threshold\nThis query detects literals that look like Solorigate Hash values used in the Solorigate code.\n\nThis query detects when the code includes at least 5 of the Hash literals used in the Solorigate tampered code\n\nPlease notice that by themselves these literals are not malign, but several of the values together would be less likely to be coincidental.\n\nThe nation-state supply chain attack on SolarWinds known as Solorigate or SunBurst gave nation-state actors access to some victims' networks.\n\nThe purpose of these rules is to identify potentially tampered code that requires further analysis.\n\n\n## Recommendation\nAny findings from this rule are only intended to indicate suspicious code that shares similarities with known portions of code used for the Solorigate attack. There is no certainty that the code is related or that the code is part of any attack.\n\nFor more information, please visit https://aka.ms/solorigate.\n\n",
                "text": "# Number of Solorigate-related hashes as literals is above the threshold\nThis query detects literals that look like Solorigate Hash values used in the Solorigate code.\n\nThis query detects when the code includes at least 5 of the Hash literals used in the Solorigate tampered code\n\nPlease notice that by themselves these literals are not malign, but several of the values together would be less likely to be coincidental.\n\nThe nation-state supply chain attack on SolarWinds known as Solorigate or SunBurst gave nation-state actors access to some victims' networks.\n\nThe purpose of these rules is to identify potentially tampered code that requires further analysis.\n\n\n## Recommendation\nAny findings from this rule are only intended to indicate suspicious code that shares similarities with known portions of code used for the Solorigate attack. There is no certainty that the code is related or that the code is part of any attack.\n\nFor more information, please visit https://aka.ms/solorigate.\n\n"
              },
              "id": "cs/solorigate/number-of-known-hashes-above-threshold",
              "name": "cs/solorigate/number-of-known-hashes-above-threshold",
              "properties": {
                "description": "The total number of Solorigate-related hash literals found in the code is above a threshold, which may indicate that an external agent has tampered with the code.\n      It is recommended to review the code and verify that there is no unexpected code in this project, however it is highly unlikely the hash values would be present coincidentally.",
                "id": "cs/solorigate/number-of-known-hashes-above-threshold",
                "kind": "problem",
                "name": "Number of Solorigate-related hashes as literals is above the threshold",
                "precision": "medium",
                "problem.severity": "warning",
                "queryURI": "https://github.com/github/codeql/blob/a1cdf256ad6b7c3e9984db9069671647e5f47921/csharp/ql/src/experimental/Security%20Features/campaign/Solorigate/NumberOfKnownHashesAboveThreshold.ql",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "solorigate"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Number of Solorigate-related hashes as literals is above the threshold"
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "enabled": true,
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "URL forward based on unvalidated user-input may cause file information disclosure."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# Unsafe url forward from remote source\nConstructing a server-side redirect path with user input could allow an attacker to download application binaries (including application classes or jar files) or view arbitrary files within protected directories.\n\n\n## Recommendation\nIn order to prevent untrusted URL forwarding, it is recommended to avoid concatenating user input directly into the forwarding URL.\n\n\n## Example\nThe following examples show the bad case and the good case respectively. The `bad` methods show an HTTP request parameter being used directly in a URL forward without validating the input, which may cause file leakage. In `good1` method, ordinary forwarding requests are shown, which will not cause file leakage.\n\n\n```java\nimport java.io.IOException;\nimport javax.servlet.ServletException;\nimport javax.servlet.http.HttpServletRequest;\nimport javax.servlet.http.HttpServletResponse;\nimport org.springframework.stereotype.Controller;\nimport org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.GetMapping;\nimport org.springframework.web.servlet.ModelAndView;\n\n@Controller\npublic class UnsafeUrlForward {\n\n\t@GetMapping(\"/bad1\")\n\tpublic ModelAndView bad1(String url) {\n\t\treturn new ModelAndView(url);\n\t}\n\n\t@GetMapping(\"/bad2\")\n\tpublic void bad2(String url, HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response) {\n\t\ttry {\n\t\t\trequest.getRequestDispatcher(\"/WEB-INF/jsp/\" + url + \".jsp\").include(request, response);\n\t\t} catch (ServletException e) {\n\t\t\te.printStackTrace();\n\t\t} catch (IOException e) {\n\t\t\te.printStackTrace();\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\t@GetMapping(\"/good1\")\n\tpublic void good1(String url, HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response) {\n\t\ttry {\n\t\t\trequest.getRequestDispatcher(\"/index.jsp?token=\" + url).forward(request, response);\n\t\t} catch (ServletException e) {\n\t\t\te.printStackTrace();\n\t\t} catch (IOException e) {\n\t\t\te.printStackTrace();\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n\n```\n\n## References\n* File Disclosure: [Unsafe Url Forward](https://vulncat.fortify.com/en/detail?id=desc.dataflow.java.file_disclosure_spring).\n",
                "text": "# Unsafe url forward from remote source\nConstructing a server-side redirect path with user input could allow an attacker to download application binaries (including application classes or jar files) or view arbitrary files within protected directories.\n\n\n## Recommendation\nIn order to prevent untrusted URL forwarding, it is recommended to avoid concatenating user input directly into the forwarding URL.\n\n\n## Example\nThe following examples show the bad case and the good case respectively. The `bad` methods show an HTTP request parameter being used directly in a URL forward without validating the input, which may cause file leakage. In `good1` method, ordinary forwarding requests are shown, which will not cause file leakage.\n\n\n```java\nimport java.io.IOException;\nimport javax.servlet.ServletException;\nimport javax.servlet.http.HttpServletRequest;\nimport javax.servlet.http.HttpServletResponse;\nimport org.springframework.stereotype.Controller;\nimport org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.GetMapping;\nimport org.springframework.web.servlet.ModelAndView;\n\n@Controller\npublic class UnsafeUrlForward {\n\n\t@GetMapping(\"/bad1\")\n\tpublic ModelAndView bad1(String url) {\n\t\treturn new ModelAndView(url);\n\t}\n\n\t@GetMapping(\"/bad2\")\n\tpublic void bad2(String url, HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response) {\n\t\ttry {\n\t\t\trequest.getRequestDispatcher(\"/WEB-INF/jsp/\" + url + \".jsp\").include(request, response);\n\t\t} catch (ServletException e) {\n\t\t\te.printStackTrace();\n\t\t} catch (IOException e) {\n\t\t\te.printStackTrace();\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\t@GetMapping(\"/good1\")\n\tpublic void good1(String url, HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response) {\n\t\ttry {\n\t\t\trequest.getRequestDispatcher(\"/index.jsp?token=\" + url).forward(request, response);\n\t\t} catch (ServletException e) {\n\t\t\te.printStackTrace();\n\t\t} catch (IOException e) {\n\t\t\te.printStackTrace();\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n\n```\n\n## References\n* File Disclosure: [Unsafe Url Forward](https://vulncat.fortify.com/en/detail?id=desc.dataflow.java.file_disclosure_spring).\n"
              },
              "id": "java/unsafe-url-forward",
              "name": "java/unsafe-url-forward",
              "properties": {
                "description": "URL forward based on unvalidated user-input\n              may cause file information disclosure.",
                "id": "java/unsafe-url-forward",
                "kind": "path-problem",
                "name": "Unsafe url forward from remote source",
                "precision": "high",
                "problem.severity": "error",
                "queryURI": "https://github.com/github/codeql/blob/a1cdf256ad6b7c3e9984db9069671647e5f47921/java/ql/src/experimental/Security/CWE/CWE-552/UnsafeUrlForward.ql",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "external/cwe-552"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Unsafe url forward from remote source"
              }
            },
